<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-open-and-closed-sets-in-metric-subspaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Open and closed sets in metric subspaces
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m> U \subseteq X </m> be a subset of a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that a subset <m> V \subseteq U </m> is open in the metric subspace <m> \of{ U , \rho } </m> if and only if <m> V = U \cap W </m> for some open set <m> W \subseteq X </m> in <m> X </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        We first observe that for each positive real number <m> r \gt 0 </m> and point <m> c \in U </m>, there are two possibly distinct sets with equal claims to the notation <m> \mB_{ r } \of{ c } </m>. Specifically, we may consider the open ball of radius <m> r </m> and center <m> c </m> in the metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m> or the open ball of the same radius and center in the metric subspace <m> \of{ U , \rho } </m>. The key issue is that these open balls need not agree; that is,
        
        <md>
          \setmid{ u \in U }{ \rho \of{ u , c } \lt r } \subseteq \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , c } \lt r }
        </md>,
        
        but this containment might be strict. As such, we will avoid the notation <m> \mB_{ r } \of{ c } </m> in the following argument and instead describe the relevant open balls with set-builder notation.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        First suppose that <m> V </m> is open in <m> U </m>. Then <m> V </m> is a neighborhood of each of its points, and so contains a metric open ball of positive radius centered at each of its points. In particular, for each point <m> v \in V </m> there is a positive real number <m> r_{ v } \gt 0 </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \setmid{ u \in U }{ \rho \of{ u , v } \lt r_{ v } } \subseteq V
        </md>.
        
        Let
        
        <md>
          W = \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r_{ v } } }
        </md>.
        
        <m> W </m> is a union of metric open balls in <m> X </m>, and so <m> W </m> is open in <m> X </m> by <xref ref="lemma-open-balls-and-closed-disks"/> and (2) of <xref ref="proposition-topology-of-a-metric-space"/>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            U \cap W \amp = U \cap \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r_{ v } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ U \cap \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r_{ v } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ \setmid{ u \in U }{ \rho \of{ u , v } \lt r_{ v } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp \subseteq \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ V } = V
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            V \amp = \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ V } \subseteq \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ \setmid{ u \in U }{ \rho \of{ u , v } \lt r_{ v } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ U \cap \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r_{ v } } }
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = U \cap \bigcup_{ v \in V }{ \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r_{ v } } } = U \cap W
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        Thus <m> V = U \cap W </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> V = U \cap W </m> for some open set <m> W \subseteq X </m> in <m> X </m>, and let <m> v \in V </m>. Then <m> v \in W </m>, and so <m> W </m> is a neighborhood of <m> v </m> in <m> X </m>. In particular,
        
        <md>
          \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r } \subseteq W
        </md>
        
        for some positive real number <m> r \gt 0 </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          \setmid{ u \in U }{ \rho \of{ u , v } \lt r } = U \cap \setmid{ x \in X }{ \rho \of{ x , v } \lt r } \subseteq U \cap W = V
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> V </m> is a neighborhood of <m> v </m> in <m> U </m>. Since this holds for all points <m> v \in V </m>, <m> v </m> is open in <m> U </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that if a subset <m> V \subseteq U </m> is open in <m> X </m>, then it is open in <m> U </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        If <m> V </m> is open in <m> X </m>, then <m> V = U \cap V </m> is open in <m> U </m> by (a) above.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Construct an explicit counterexample to the converse of (b) above.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        The singleton set <m> V = \set{ 0 } </m> is open in the metric subspace <m> U = \Z </m> of <m> X = \R </m>, but <m> V = \set{ 0 } </m> is not open in <m> X = \R </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
</exercise>
